1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,325
How to build an Agentforce agent in Zana.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:10,038
Use Pi with the SF-Pi plugin. It builds, previews, and tests the agent for you.

3
00:00:11,301 --> 00:00:17,333
Start a thread on the Pi harness, in your Salesforce project, and describe the
agent you want.

4
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:24,140
Pi plans it, writes the Agent Script, and compiles it, right in the thread.

5
00:00:24,323 --> 00:00:26,585
It hands back a card for the new agent.

6
00:00:26,765 --> 00:00:32,860
Click it, and the agent opens beside the thread: the script, and its
conversation graph.

7
00:00:33,041 --> 00:00:38,346
Ask what the return window is. Pi previews the agent and links the run.

8
00:00:39,012 --> 00:00:44,800
The run opens in the same panel. Fourteen days. Our policy is thirty.

9
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:52,111
Ask for thirty days, and five test scenarios. Pi edits the file, then writes the
scenarios itself.

10
00:00:52,292 --> 00:00:54,829
Open it again, and the script reads thirty.

11
00:00:55,009 --> 00:01:01,612
Pi runs the five scenarios as simulated customers, four at a time. Five of five
pass.

12
00:01:01,794 --> 00:01:04,754
Check it yourself, right in the panel: thirty days.

13
00:01:08,213 --> 00:01:15,148
When you want the full workbench, open it in Agentforce Studio: the same script,
thirty days and all.

14
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:21,278
Every action is one click away, like the return Flow, drawn as its real canvas.

15
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:26,168
And the Lightning Type the order lookup returns, with the card that renders it.

16
00:01:26,467 --> 00:01:31,652
Here's a separate test run with a failing return policy. Ask the agent to
improve it.

17
00:01:32,288 --> 00:01:37,182
Two pass. The judge shows exactly why the return policy failed.

18
00:01:37,365 --> 00:01:44,156
Ask it to improve the failing one. It tests every attempt against the same
batch, without touching your file.

19
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:51,623
Baseline two of three. Best, three of three.

20
00:01:51,804 --> 00:01:56,040
You review the change as a diff. Accept it, and save.

21
00:01:56,665 --> 00:02:01,873
Ask it to check again: thirty days. And the batch now passes, three of three.

22
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:08,004
Your editor can evolve, too. Ask your coding agent to add a missing editor tool.

23
00:02:08,436 --> 00:02:13,246
It updates the editor, checks the change, and reloads the plugin in place.

24
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:17,069
The export tool appears in the toolbar. No reinstall.

25
00:02:17,425 --> 00:02:22,111
Click it to export this agent's diagnostics, including its test results.

26
00:02:22,464 --> 00:02:26,593
Keep working in the same editor, with the new tool ready to use.
